From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:55:43 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: keep host-object debug information out of BTF

Host objects are compiled against the host C library. Its headers declare
types under the names the kernel uses for its own types, such as size_t,
pid_t, sigset_t and struct sigaction, with host definitions; on Darwin they
resolve to __darwin_* types. pahole encodes the DWARF of every compilation
unit, so those host definitions entered vmlinux BTF beside the kernel's.

Compile host objects without debug information when BTF is generated.
Kernel objects keep their DWARF, crash symbolization uses System.map, and
builds without BTF keep host-object debug information.
---
 arch/um/Makefile | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/um/Makefile b/arch/um/Makefile
index f52bebee8c4bd12f2a52dca08a5f51dbe7bb6808..2aecce6eb3a8893eb84cabf125e48e4ce5c586a5 100644
--- a/arch/um/Makefile
+++ b/arch/um/Makefile
@@ -78,6 +78,14 @@ USER_CFLAGS = $(patsubst $(KERNEL_DEFINES),,$(patsubst -I%,,$(KBUILD_CFLAGS))) \
 		-include $(srctree)/include/linux/compiler-version.h \
 		-include $(srctree)/include/linux/kconfig.h
 
+# Host objects are compiled against the host C library, whose types reuse the
+# kernel's names with host definitions (size_t, pid_t, sigset_t, struct
+# sigaction, ...). pahole would encode that DWARF into the kernel's BTF, so
+# omit debug information from host objects when BTF is generated.
+ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF
+USER_CFLAGS += -g0
+endif
+
 # This adjusts flags for the host operating system.  Upstream UML only has a
 # Linux host backend; the Darwin backend deliberately keeps that selection
 # explicit so Linux builds remain unchanged.
